     "i": "ti-list-check",
     "h": "<div class=\"pblk\"><b>What it is and why it matters.</b> The presentation layer on top of the Demand Mapping. Two goals: inform the client on nature market potential and land access, and build the relationship as GY's first chance to show its expertise. The job is already won, so the intro shows GY has understood the client's vision and ambition; it is not a pitch. (Source: Fran, 29 Oct. Confidence: high)</div><div class=\"pblk\"><b>Users</b></div><div class=\"dtwrap\"><table class=\"dtbl\"><thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Part they play</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"m\"><span class=\"ph\"><i>role or person</i></span></td><td><span class=\"ph\"><i>makes / reviews / reads / decides</i></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"pblk\"><b>Resources.</b> The main input is the <a class=\"nlink\" href=\"https://app.notion.com/p/4078a14ed56a81bf824fc1873c0c10c5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Customer Demand Mapping Blueprint</a> (v0.4), one run per estate or site. Other inputs: the client's own documents, the ecosystem service rule books, Harry's gy-commercial-strategy skill (standard GY framing text), current market data (source to confirm) and Fran's judgement. (Source: Customer Demand Mapping Blueprint v0.4; Granola 14 and 29 Oct. Confidence: high)</div><div class=\"pblk\"><b>What the Demand Mapping covers</b></div><div class=\"dtwrap\"><table class=\"dtbl\"><thead><tr><th>Commercial Strategy section</th><th>From Demand Mapping?</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"m\">Part B: estate context, vision, pipeline</td><td>Yes, Chapter 1</td><td>Tested on Walkham; light rewrite into prose</td></tr><tr><td class=\"m\">Part B: strategic customer targets</td><td>Yes, Chapter 2</td><td>Shortlist only, grouped by buyer type; the long list stays in the Demand Mapping</td></tr><tr><td class=\"m\">Part B: supply and demand insights</td><td>Partly</td><td>Demand from Chapter 2 verdicts; supply needs Chapter 3 (untested)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"m\">Part B: opportunity (volumes, revenue, costs)</td><td>Only if Chapter 3 works</td><td>Chapter 3 untested; ECT GPAPs not yet received</td></tr><tr><td class=\"m\">Part B: risks and dependencies</td><td>Partly</td><td>Chapter 1 constraints and open questions</td></tr><tr><td class=\"m\">Part B: estate blended finance, recommendations</td><td>Removed (trial)</td><td>Out of scope from v0.2 (Fran, 29 Oct)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"m\">Part A: market primers (introduction, MRV, routes)</td><td>No</td><td>Mostly standard GY text from the skill and the ES rule books</td></tr><tr><td class=\"m\">Part A: market status and pricing</td><td>No</td><td>Needs a current market data source</td></tr><tr><td class=\"m\">Part A: stacking and bundling</td><td>No</td><td>Standard GY text plus client-specific choices; governance, blended finance and private capital removed (trial, v0.2)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"m\">Part A: executive summary</td><td>No</td><td>Written last</td></tr><tr><td class=\"m\">Part C: next steps, conclusion</td><td>No</td><td>Expert judgement; rounds back to the vision, no recommendations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"pblk\">Rough share: Demand Mapping Chapters 1 and 2 alone give about a quarter of the document; with Chapter 3 working, about 40 to 50% by effort, mostly Part B. The Commercial Strategy workflow should therefore focus on assembling Part A, refreshing market status, choosing the buyer shortlist and the expert's next steps. (Inferred: from comparing the two Blueprints' templates, confidence medium)</div><div class=\"pblk\"><b>Constraints.</b> <i>Regulation, buyer diligence, timing.</i></div>"
