     "h": "<div class=\"pblk\"><b>Type.</b> Document (Google Doc, then GY-branded Word via gy-document-designer). Starting point: Fran's <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1J4GwrajTrV3yV6OOqmP3TWgwvmg5hPVo9suAyZmsg9I/edit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ECT draft v0.1</a> skeleton. (Source: Thomas, 29 Oct. Confidence: high)</div><div class=\"pblk\"><b>Front matter.</b> Title, client, version and date line, confidentiality; a status note (what this version contains); a legend for gap markers [BLANK] and [Comment: ...]; numbered source references [S1], [S2] pointing to a Sources list at the end. (Source: ECT draft v0.1. Confidence: high)</div><div class=\"pblk\"><b>Part A: Shared portfolio strategy</b> (written once per client)</div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">1.</span><span>Executive Summary: key messages; portfolio summary; questions for the client to consider</span></div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">2.</span><span>Introduction: client vision; landholding and the case for nature markets; ecosystem services: identifying a natural capital opportunity; market readiness; demand mapping methodology</span></div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">3.</span><span>Nature markets: shared market primers, one per service in scope (Carbon; BNG; Voluntary Biodiversity; NFM; Water Quality and Nutrient Mitigation; Replenish). Each primer: Market Introduction, Market Status, MRV (measurement, reporting and verification), Pricing Summary, Routes to Market</span></div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">4.</span><span>Ground rules: stacking and bundling</span></div><div class=\"pblk\"><i>Governance, blended finance (incl. public funding and BFP) and private capital removed as a trial from v0.2: risk of giving away too much, atypical for commercial strategies (originated from Upper Dee). See Challenge.</i></div><div class=\"pblk\"><b>Part B: Estate strategies</b> (one or more estates or sites, one chapter each, same sub-order each time; the single client vision sits in Part A and threads through every chapter)</div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">&bull;</span><span>Estate context</span></div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">&bull;</span><span>Interventions, with sub-headings set to the estate's own interventions (no separate vision per estate)</span></div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">&bull;</span><span>Natural capital pipeline</span></div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">&bull;</span><span>One section per service in scope for that estate: Supply and Demand Insights; [Estate] [Service] Opportunity (carbon adds Woodland or Peatland Carbon and Indicative Costs of Restoration; NFM adds Proposed NFM Interventions); Strategic Customer Targets (shortlist only, grouped by type: public sector, local carbon, academic, national); a short estate-specific Routes to Market note where markets differ</span></div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">&bull;</span><span>Risks and dependencies</span></div><div class=\"pblk\"><b>Part C: Close</b></div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">&bull;</span><span>GY proposed next steps</span></div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">&bull;</span><span>Conclusion: rounds back to the client's vision; no specific recommendations</span></div><div class=\"bul\"><span class=\"dot\">&bull;</span><span>Sources</span></div><div class=\"pblk\"><i>Key change from Harry's skill: the eight per-service sub-sections are split in two. Market-wide parts (introduction, status, MRV, pricing, routes) sit once in Part A; site-specific parts (supply and demand, opportunity, customer targets) repeat per estate in Part B. A single-site client has one Part B chapter.</i> (Inferred: from comparing ECT draft v0.1 headings with the gy-commercial-strategy skill, confidence medium)</div>"
